ABSTRACTS = [
'''Skill Bridge is a community employment and skill matching portal developed as part of our Community Engagement Project at Khalsa College, Matunga. Our team completed three visits to the college and gathered real student inputs by manually circulating a Google Form. The project addresses the need to connect opportunity information, student interests, skill preparation and placement follow-up in a clear workflow. Its objective is to provide useful student services while giving coordinators a separate workspace for operational decisions.

The application provides profiles, opportunity browsing, explained matching, saved opportunities, applications, workshop enrolment, feedback and guidance requests. Coordinator functions include managing listings, reviewing applications, maintaining training records and responding to requests. Administrative functions cover protected content, diagnostic queries and backup restoration. Public research views use aggregate information, while access to individual survey responses is restricted.

The implementation uses React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS and Recharts for the frontend, with an Express server and SQLite persistence. Matching is a deterministic weighted process based on profile and opportunity fields. It returns reasons for its assessment and does not predict the probability of employment. Server-side authorization, input validation and database constraints support reliable operation.

The latest recorded verification passed 157 automated checks across acceptance, workflow, lifecycle and production-serving suites. The TypeScript check and production build also passed. These results establish the tested technical behaviour, including fresh production startup and administrator operations. They do not establish a measured improvement in placement outcomes. The portal provides a practical basis for organised placement support; verified survey totals, signed user acceptance and measured institutional impact remain to be added to the final evidence record.''',
'''This Community Engagement Project presents Skill Bridge, a web portal intended to make placement support easier to follow for students and coordinators. The fieldwork was carried out through three visits to Khalsa College, Matunga. We collected genuine responses by manually sharing a Google Form among students. The work focuses on the connection between understanding student needs and providing a system through which opportunities, applications, training and guidance can be managed.

Students use a personal workspace to maintain profile information, explore openings, read matching explanations, apply for opportunities and enrol in workshops. They can also submit feedback and request guidance. Coordinators receive separate management views for reviewing applications and handling operational work. Administrators maintain protected configuration and recovery functions. The access model is enforced through server checks rather than interface visibility alone.

The portal uses React and TypeScript, supported by Vite, Tailwind CSS and Recharts. Express provides the API, and SQLite stores related records. A rule-based scoring process compares department, role preference, skills, location and a detected academic cutoff. The output is advisory: it explains relevance without claiming to determine employer selection.

Technical validation included compilation, isolated workflows, database lifecycle checks and production serving. The latest recorded run passed 157 automated checks, along with TypeScript and the production build. Lint completed with warnings. The results show that the tested functions operate as expected, but direct user acceptance and live-host verification remain separate activities. Skill Bridge offers a structured support mechanism aligned with learning and employment preparation. Confirmed field-response statistics, signed feedback and evidence of organisational adoption are to be added from the original records.''',
'''Skill Bridge was developed to connect student placement activities with coordinator follow-up through a shared web application. The project formed part of our CEP work at Khalsa College, Matunga, where all three team visits took place. Student information was gathered manually through a circulated Google Form. The purpose was to support a more connected process for opportunity discovery, preparation, applications and requests for assistance, while keeping responsibilities and access distinct.

The solution contains student profiles, opportunity listings, saved records, applications, workshop participation, feedback and guidance. Coordinators manage the operational queues and record decisions. Administrators handle protected system documents, approved diagnostics and backup restoration. Research information is presented publicly through summaries, with individual respondent records limited to authorized staff. Persistent records allow actions and decisions to be retrieved across sessions.

The technical structure consists of a React and TypeScript frontend, an Express API and a SQLite database. Vite prepares the frontend build, Tailwind CSS provides styling and Recharts supports charts. Matching uses fixed weighted comparisons rather than machine learning. It provides reasons and eligibility cautions so that students can interpret the result instead of treating it as a hiring prediction.

The final recorded verification passed 37 acceptance checks, 97 workflow and security checks, 14 lifecycle checks and 9 production-serving checks. The build and TypeScript checks also passed. This evidence supports the tested local operation of the portal, including production startup and administration. It does not replace field-based user acceptance or demonstrate improved placement rates. The project delivers a practical system and a documented technical baseline. Verified survey analysis, visit evidence and formal acceptance records remain necessary supporting material for the final submission.''',
'''Our Community Engagement Project resulted in Skill Bridge, a portal for student employment preparation and placement coordination. The team visited Khalsa College, Matunga, three times and gathered real student responses by manually distributing a Google Form. The project’s central concern is continuity: students should be able to move from finding an opportunity to taking action and receiving a response, while staff should have an organised view of the work requiring attention.

The application separates student, placement coordinator and administrator access. Its functions include profile management, opportunity discovery, explained matching, application tracking, workshops, feedback, guidance and research summaries. Coordinator actions update shared operational records, while administrative tools support configuration and data recovery. Individual survey responses are restricted, and public reporting uses aggregate information.

React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS and Recharts form the frontend stack. The backend uses Express and SQLite. Matching is implemented as a deterministic scoring function that compares selected profile and listing attributes. Because it is a relevance aid rather than a predictive model, the interface also provides reasons and eligibility information. Authentication, server-side permissions, validation and relational constraints support the main workflows.

The latest recorded test run passed 157 checks across four suites, and both the production build and TypeScript verification succeeded. Fresh production serving, administrator login, an authorized update and logout were included in the checks. These results describe technical behaviour under the test conditions. They do not demonstrate live institutional adoption, performance under load or a change in employment outcomes. The project offers a usable foundation for coordinated placement support. Original survey totals, photographs, signed feedback and user-acceptance results are to be attached as the remaining fieldwork evidence.'''
]
